From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 17:14:56 +0200
Subject: watchdog: intel_grx500: follow the counter clock when its rate
 changes

GIC_Vx_WD_COUNT0 is clocked by the CPU clock.  The driver measures that
rate once at probe and derives the hardware window, max_hw_heartbeat_ms
and the reported pretimeout from it, so all of it goes stale the moment
anything moves that clock -- and get_timeleft() then reports a number
wrong by the whole ratio.

Measured on an FRITZ!Box 7590, stepping the CPU divider through the
vendor OPPs with the watchdog armed by the core throughout:

  PLL0B_CFG1[15:12]    nominal      counter rate    ratio    error
  0x1 (/2)           1000.00 MHz   1000171487 Hz   1.00000   +171 ppm
  0x2 (/3)            666.67 MHz    666456864 Hz   0.66634   -315 ppm
  0x5 (/6)            333.33 MHz    333339423 Hz   0.33328    +18 ppm
  0x1 (/2) restored  1000.00 MHz    999969145 Hz   0.99980    -31 ppm

The counter lands on the CGU-derived CPU frequency to within 315 ppm at
every divider setting.  timeleft meanwhile reported the same {2, 3, 4}
second values at all three rates: at 333 MHz it claims 2-4 s remaining
where the real window is 7.1-12.9 s, a threefold error on the defect's
own public surface, with nothing in the reading to hint at it.

Nothing on the device can see this happen.  CP0 count runs on the same
clock and mips_hpt_frequency is sampled once, with the cevt-r4k rescaler
compiled out for want of CONFIG_CPU_FREQ, so /proc/uptime tracked the
counter to five decimal places at every step (1.000028, 0.666684,
0.333375, 1.000053 against host wall clock).  The board lost 15.5 s of
wall clock in one step and 29.9 s in the next and never noticed.  That
is also why the new rate has to come from the notifier: re-running
grx500_wdt_measure_rate() would compare the moved counter against an
equally moved reference and return the old answer.

So register a clk rate-change notifier, POST_RATE_CHANGE only, in the
shape drivers/clocksource/mips-gic-timer.c uses for the same "this
counter runs on the CPU clock" problem: take the rate from
cnd->new_rate, recompute the derived values, and reload the countdown in
the new tick domain.  The clk handle moves out of probe into the driver
struct so it can be kept, and the derived values move into one helper
now that they have to be computed twice.

No PRE arm and no NOTIFY_BAD.  The core's keepalive hrtimer is driven by
the same clock, so the feed interval and the counter rate scale
together: the drain per feed cycle came out at 0.435 to 0.439 of the
counter's full range at ratios 1, 1.5 and 3, and the counter floor never
approached zero in any of them.  NOTIFY_BAD would in exchange abort the
transition for the entire clock subtree and hand -EBUSY to a caller with
no interest in the watchdog, to guard a floor that no rate below
4.29 GHz can reach.

The re-arm is conditional on watchdog_hw_running(), because the core
clears WDOG_HW_RUNNING before it calls .stop and an unconditional arm
here would restart a watchdog that userspace had stopped.

This is vendor-inherited rather than a porting regression: AVM's 4.9
grx500_wdt.c carries no clk or cpufreq notifier either, and that BSP
ships cpufreq enabled.  Our port is safe today only because it enables
none.

While here, relabel the probe log fields that called clk_get_rate()'s
result "DT".  There is no DT rate property anywhere in this driver.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

